prompt:
Write a method named `sumList` that takes an `ArrayList<Integer>` parameter `list` and returns the sum of all its elements. For example, if `list` contains `3, 5, 2`, the method should return `10`.

Complete the method below.

```java
public int sumList(ArrayList<Integer> list) {
    /* your code here */
}
```

sampleResponse:
```java
public int sumList(ArrayList<Integer> list) {
    int sum = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < list.size(); i++) {
        sum += list.get(i);
    }
    return sum;
}
```

prompt:
Write a method named `removeNegatives` that takes an `ArrayList<Integer>` parameter `list` and removes every negative value from it, modifying `list` in place. Traverse the list backward to safely remove elements without skipping any.

Complete the method below.

```java
public void removeNegatives(ArrayList<Integer> list) {
    /* your code here */
}
```

sampleResponse:
```java
public void removeNegatives(ArrayList<Integer> list) {
    for (int i = list.size() - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
        if (list.get(i) < 0) {
            list.remove(i);
        }
    }
}
```

prompt:
Write a method named `doubleAll` that takes an `ArrayList<Integer>` parameter `list` and replaces each element with double its original value, modifying `list` in place. For example, if `list` contains `3, 5, 2`, after calling `doubleAll(list)` it should contain `6, 10, 4`.

Complete the method below.

```java
public void doubleAll(ArrayList<Integer> list) {
    /* your code here */
}
```

sampleResponse:
```java
public void doubleAll(ArrayList<Integer> list) {
    for (int i = 0; i < list.size(); i++) {
        list.set(i, list.get(i) * 2);
    }
}
```
